    "username": "vasuman",
    "nickname": "Vasu",
    "text": "I think you’re confusing the ultimate destination with the journey. AI transformation is a continuum and also a journey, not binary. Setting such an absolutist bar discounts meaningful progress. Also it seems like you are really focused on architectural sophistication. Shouldn’t outcomes and value generated be the way you measure success?",
    "quoted_tweet": null,
    "url": "https://x.com/RMB/status/2114126731221102760",
    "time": "2026-10-25 06:47",
    "created_at": "Sat Oct 24 22:47:45 +0000 2026",
    "id_str": "2114126731221102760",
    "is_retweet": true
  },
  {
    "username": "vasuman",
    "nickname": "Vasu",
    "text": "Not confused 😁 I agree it's a journey. A few points:\n\n1. Most firms doing skill files and training workshops call that the destination. They never graduate their clients to more. They do it on purpose because doing real AI Transformation is much harder and takes far longer. \n\n2. Skill files and workshops do move P&L, but marginally. Single-digit movement vs double-digit with real AI transformation.\n\n3. Outcomes and value generated are the right measure of success. They're also hard to measure, and most of these firms don't measure them properly. Token spend, skill usage and \"agents built\" are the wrong metrics. Measuring value properly needs a baseline, and you can't set one without deep discovery up front.\n\nThis is why we start every engagement with a pilot, where we:\n- Baseline the KPIs\n- Map the current process and redesign it for the future state\n- Build agent POCs across the stack, on the systems of record, to show what's possible\n- Agree the value at stake with the client, so we hold ourselves to a positive ROI",
    "quoted_tweet": null,
    "url": "https://x.com/vasuman/status/2114140498919395374",
    "time": "2026-10-25 07:42",
    "created_at": "Sat Oct 24 23:42:28 +0000 2026",
    "id_str": "2114140498919395374",
    "is_retweet": false
  },
